findings.evidence:
The route takes an identifier and the identifier is never authorized:

  api/resources.py:29-35
      class CertificateTags(AuthenticatedResource):
          def post(self, certificate_id, data=None):
              for tag in data["tags"]:
                  permission = TagPermission(tag.id, service.current_user())
                  if not permission.can():
                      return dict(message="You are not authorized to use this tag"), 403
              return service.attach_tags(certificate_id, data["tags"])

certificate_id arrives from the path, is compared with nothing and is handed to service.attach_tags with no principal beside it. The check the handler does run is on the other object and cannot fail: api/schemas.py:16-17 has already rebuilt data["tags"] out of service.current_user().tags, so every element is a row the caller owns and TagPermission.can() (api/permissions.py:20-21, any(t.id == self.tag_id for t in self.user.tags)) returns True for all of them. The module knows how to scope a read when it means to - api/resources.py:15 passes service.current_user() into service.list_for - and no equivalent appears on this write. Certificate carries an owner (api/models.py:9), so the row addressed by certificate_id belongs to somebody.
